    "description": "The administrative console exposes account review, session revocation, and role assignment screens through Selenium tests. Dozens of security regression tests currently locate labels and buttons, wait for status text, and interpret banner messages directly in each test body. A markup redesign therefore breaks the entire suite and obscures which user action each test performs. Create one wrapper for the account review screen and another for the session administration screen. Each wrapper owns all selectors, waits, and driver interactions, and exposes operations such as searchByEmail, openReview, assignLeastPrivilegeRole, and readOutcome. Tests should call those operations and assert on observable review results, while the wrappers remain responsible for synchronization and page-specific mechanics. The console has no reusable component already hiding these details, and the wrappers must not contain authorization policy decisions.",
    "decision_statement": "Choose whether to isolate console locators and interactions behind screen-specific test wrappers instead of embedding them in security regression tests.",
    "notes": "Console locators and interactions move behind screen-specific wrappers exposing user operations (page-object); arrange-act-assert is the near-miss for test structure, with test-data-builder supporting review-data setup."
  },
